o A has one favorable comparison, B has zero. Thus, the reason would prefer A. But the report does not mention this. So the reason is applicable: it can be applied by comparing max and min outcomes, and determining which lottery has more favorable comparisons (here, A has one favorable: max > B's max; B has none in max or min). Thus, the decision reason is applicable to the problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'Ich nehme B da die Wahrscheinlichkeit mit 50% überhaupt Geld zu erhalten höher ist als bei A wo ich nur 25% habe. Dann lieber weniger Geld aber höhrer Chance überhaupt was zu bekommen.' Translation: 'I choose B because the probability of getting any money is 50% in B, compared to 25% in A. I prefer getting less money but with a higher chance of getting anything at all.' The individual focuses on the probability of receiving any money (i.e., positive outcome), not on comparing maximum or minimum monetary outcomes. The individual considers only the probability of receiving at least some money (i.e., non-zero outcome). Specifically, in A, the chance of getting money is 25% (only 6000 with 25%), in B, it is 50% (25% + 25% = 50% chance of getting 4000 or 2000). The individual justifies the choice by emphasizing the higher probability of receiving any money, even at the cost of lower expected value or lower maximum payoff. The deliberation process centers on risk of getting zero versus getting positive amount, not on comparing max/min outcomes or counting favorable comparisons between lotteries. Thus, the individual does not consider max or min outcomes in a comparative fashion, nor does the individual mention any comparison of outcome levels (e.g., A has higher max, B has higher middle). The justification is based solely on probability of receiving any money, not on a rule involving favorable comparisons of max/min outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires comparing max and min outcomes and counting favorable comparisons (e.g., A has higher max, B has higher middle). In this case, A has a higher maximum (6000 vs. 4000), and B has a higher middle value (2000 vs. 0). So there are two favorable comparisons: A > B in max, B > A in middle. But the reason specifies preferring the lottery with a higher number of favorable comparisons. Since both have one favorable comparison each (A in max, B in middle), the reason would be indifferent. However, the reason does not say it prefers the one with more favorable comparisons in a tie — it says it prefers the one with a higher number. If both have one, then no preference. But the individual does not consider max or min comparisons at all. Instead, the individual focuses entirely on the probability of getting any money (50% vs. 25%). Therefore, the information considered by the individual (probability of positive outcome) is not aligned with the decision reason's focus (comparisons of max and min outcomes). Furthermore, the justification (preferring higher chance of getting money) is not based on a rule involving favorable comparisons of outcomes. Thus, the individual did not use the decision reason. The reason is not present in the deliberation process. The individual's reasoning is based on a different criterion (probability of positive outcome), not on comparing max/min and counting favorable comparisons. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
